.TH WEBSOCKET 2
.SH NAME
websocket: dial, client, Conn, Msg \- WebSocket client (RFC 6455)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "websocket.m";
ws := load Websocket Websocket->PATH;
Conn, Msg, Header: import ws;

init:      fn(): string;
dial:      fn(url: string, hdrs: list of ref Header): (ref Conn, string);
client:    fn(fd: ref Sys->FD, url: string, hdrs: list of ref Header):
               (ref Conn, string);

Msg: adt {
    op:    int;           # Otext, Obinary or Oclose
    data:  array of byte;
    code:  int;           # Oclose: the server's close code
    text:  fn(m: self ref Msg): string;
};

Conn: adt {
    fd:        ref Sys->FD;
    url:       string;
    protocol:  string;
    headers:   list of ref Header;
    read:      fn(c: self ref Conn): (ref Msg, string);
    write:     fn(c: self ref Conn, op: int, data: array of byte): string;
    writetext: fn(c: self ref Conn, s: string): string;
    ping:      fn(c: self ref Conn, data: array of byte): string;
    close:     fn(c: self ref Conn, code: int, reason: string): string;
};

acceptkey: fn(key: string): string;
frame:     fn(fin, op: int, data, mask: array of byte): array of byte;
utf8ok:    fn(data: array of byte): int;
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Websocket
is the client end of the WebSocket protocol: a stream of whole messages
over one HTTP connection, upgraded.
Services such as Slack's Socket Mode send live events this way.
The module is a mechanism only; what the messages mean, and what is
served from them, is the caller's.
.PP
.B Init
loads the modules
.B websocket
needs and must be called first; it returns an error string, or nil.
.PP
.B Dial
connects to a
.B ws://
or
.B wss://
.I url
(TCP, or TLS verified against
.BR /lib/certs ,
through
.IR webclient (2)'s
.BR tlsdial ),
and upgrades it.
.I Hdrs
are sent with the upgrade: an
.BR Authorization ,
an
.BR Origin ,
a
.B Sec-WebSocket-Protocol
listing the subprotocols offered.
The handshake's own headers
.RB ( Host ,
.BR Upgrade ,
.BR Connection ,
.BR Sec-WebSocket-Key ,
.BR -Version ,
.BR -Extensions )
are refused, as is any name or value with a control character in it.
The server's reply must be
.B 101
with the right
.BR Sec-WebSocket-Accept ;
a subprotocol it chose is in
.BR c.protocol ,
and must have been offered.
No extension is offered, so a reply choosing one is refused.
.PP
.B Client
upgrades a connection
.I fd
already open to the URL's host: a tunnel through a proxy's
.BR CONNECT ,
a TLS connection made some other way, or a pipe in a test.
The URL gives the request's
.B Host
and target.
.PP
.B C.read
returns the next whole message: fragments are joined, a text message
must be UTF-8.
A ping is answered with a pong inside
.BR read ,
so a connection stays up only while something reads it.
When the server closes,
.B read
answers its close and returns a
.B Msg
with
.B op
.BR Oclose ,
the server's
.B code
.RB ( Cnostatus ,
1005, when it gave none) and its reason as
.BR data .
A server breaking the protocol (a masked frame, a reserved bit, a control
frame fragmented or over 125 bytes, a message over
.B MAXMSG
bytes, text that is not UTF-8, an unknown opcode) is sent a close with
the code that says how
.RB ( Cprotocol ,
.BR Cbaddata ,
.BR Ctoobig )
and
.B read
returns an error naming it.
After a close or an error the connection is finished, and
.B read
returns
.BR (nil, \&"closed") .
.PP
.B C.write
sends one message,
.B Otext
(which must be UTF-8) or
.BR Obinary ,
as one masked frame;
.B c.writetext
sends a string.
.B C.ping
sends a ping of at most 125 bytes.
.B C.close
sends a close with
.I code
(0 for none) and
.IR reason ;
the server's close then comes back from
.BR read .
Nothing may be sent after a close.
Reads and writes may be made from different threads; frames are written
one at a time.
.PP
.BR Acceptkey ,
.B frame
and
.B utf8ok
are the protocol's pieces, for tests and for a server built on this:
the
.B Sec-WebSocket-Accept
for a key, one frame
.RI ( mask
nil for a server's unmasked frame, else four bytes), and whether bytes are
well-formed UTF-8.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/lib/websocket.b
.br
.B /tests/websocket_test.b
.br
.B /tests/host/websocket_test.sh
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR webclient (2),
.IR json (2)
.br
I Fette and A Melnikov, ``The WebSocket Protocol'',
.IR RFC6455 .
.SH BUGS
The handshake waits for the server as long as it takes; a caller that
cannot wait should dial in a thread it can kill.
.B Dial
goes direct: behind an HTTP proxy, open the tunnel and use
.BR client .
